title: Harrison Ch47 Dyspnoea
---

# Ch47: Dyspnoea

## Dyspnoea

- **Definition** - subjective experience of breathing discomfort that consists of qualitatively distinct sensation that vary in intensity; the experience derives from interactions among multiple physiological, psychological, social, and environmental factors and may induce secondary physiological and behavioural responses
- **Dyspnoea vs increased work of breathing:**
  - <u>Dyspnoea</u> - subjective symptom that can be perceived only by the person experiencing it
  - <u>Increased work of breathing</u> - characterised by objective signs that are identifiable for the physician
- **Pathophysiology of dyspnoea** - consequence of interactions between sensory afferents from various receptors throughout the body and motor efferents to ventilatory muscles:
  - **Motor efferents** - increased neural output of the motor cortex due to disorders of the ventilatory pump is sensed by sensory cortex via <u>collorary discharge</u>
  - **Sensory afferents:**
    - <u>Chemoreceptors</u> - located in carotid bodies, and the medulla, activated by hypoxaemia, hypercapnia and acidaemia, producing a sensation of '**air hunger**'
    - <u>Mechanoreceptors</u> - located in airways and activated by bronchospasm (and hyperinflation), producing a sensation of **chest tightness**
    - <u>J-receptors and pulmonary vascular receptors</u> - located in pulmonary vasculature and interstitium, activated by interstitial oedema and acute changes in pulmonary vascular pressure, contributing to '**air hunger**'
    - <u>Metaboreceptors</u> - located in skeletal muscles and are activated by changes in local biochemical lilieu of tissue active during exercise
  - **Efferent-reafferent mismatch** - discrepency between feed-forward messages to ventilatory muscles and feedback from receptor results in increased intensity of dyspnoea: 
  ![](img/pathophysiological_model_of_dyspnoea_production.png)
  - **Affective component of dyspnoea** - acute anxiety or fear may increase the intensity of dyspnoea by:
    - <u>Altered perception</u> - altering the interpretation of sensory data from receptors
    - <u>Altered respiratory mechanics</u> - hyperventilation results in hyper-inflation, increasing work and effort of breathing, and the sense of an unsatisfying breath
- **Dyspnoea arises often from deviation of function from cardiovascular and respiratory system** - often these deviations produce breathlessness via:
  - <u>Increased ventilatory drive</u> - increased drive to breath
  - <u>Altered respiratory mechanics</u> - increased efforts or work of breath
  - <u>Altered feedback from sensory afferents</u> - stimulation of receptors in the heart, lungs, or vascular systems
- **DDx of dyspnoea:**
  - **Respiratory system dyspnoea** - caused by 1) disease of the the airways, 2) disease of the chest wall, and 3) disease of the lung parenchyma:
    - <u>Disease of the airways</u> - asthma, COPD
    - <u>Disease of the chest wall</u> - kyphoscoliosis, myasthenia gravis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, Pleural effusion, Pneumothorax, Atelectasis
    - <u>Disease of the lung parenchyma</u> - intesrstitial lung disease, pneumonia
  - **Cardiovascular system dyspnoea** - caused by 1) disease of the left heart, 2) disease of the pulmonary vasculature, and 3) disease of the pericardium:
    - <u>Disease of the left heart</u> - left heart failure (elevated LVEDP) from any systolic or diastolic dysfunction
    - <u>Disease of the pulmonary vasculature</u> - pulmonary embolism, primary pulmonary hypertension, and pulmonary vasculitis
    - <u>Disease of the pericardium</u> - constrictive pericarditis, cardiac tamponade
  - **Dyspnoea w/ normal respiratory and cardiovascular systems:**
    - <u>Anaemia</u> - often associated w/ exertional dyspnoea and thought to be related to stimulation of metaboreceptors
    - <u>Obesity</u> - likely as a result of increased work of breathing due to decompliance
    - <u>Cardiovascular de-conditioning</u> - due to early development of anaerobic metabolism resulting in stimulation of chemoreceptors and metaboloreceptors
    - <u>Metabolic acidosis</u> - due to stimulation of chemoreceptors
- **Primary mechanisms of dyspnoea in common diseases** - often multifactorial: 
![](img/mechanisms_of_dyspnoea_in_common_diseases.png)
  - **Disease of airways:**
    - <u>Bronchospasm</u> - stimulation of mechanoreceptors resulting in sense of chest tightness which modulates the dyspneoa experience
    - <u>Dynamic hyperinflation</u> - expiratory airflow obstruction results in progressive hyperinflation exacerbated by tachypnoea, resulting in reduced compliance, and a sensation of an unsatisfactory breath
    - <u>Hypoxaemia and hypercapnia</u> - in severe disease manifestations due to V/Q mismatch or severe airflow limitation, resulting from an increased ventilatory drive
  - **Disease of the chest wall:**
    - <u>Decreased compliance</u> - increased compliance resulting in an increased effort to breath
  - **Disease of the lung parenchyma:**
    - <u>Decreased compliance</u> - resulting in increased work of breathing
    - <u>Hypoxaemia</u> - in severe disease w/ V/Q mismatch, resulting from increased ventilatory drive
- **Cardiovascular vs respiratory dyspnoea:**
  - **Pathophysiology:**
    - <u>Cardiovascular dyspnoea</u> - causing gas-exchange abnormalities or stimulation of pulmonary vascular receptors
    - <u>Respiratory dyspnoea</u> - alteration of mechanical properties of lung-chest wall system, and stimulation of pulmonary receptors
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - **HPI** - onset, progression, quality, severity, timing:
    - <u>Onset</u> - acute vs chronic:
      - **Acute intermittent dyspnoea** - characteristic of 1) ACS/ IHD, 2) bronchospasm (asthma), 3) pulmonary embolism
      - **Chronic progressive dyspnoea** - characteristic of COPD, interstitial lung disease, congestive heart failure
    - <u>Progression</u> - static, progressive
    - <u>Quality</u> - patient asked to describe in his/her own words what the discomfort feels like: 
    ![](img/qualitative_descriptors_of_dyspnoea.png)
    - <u>Severity</u> - assessed during clinical assessment (e.g. respiratory distress, ability to complete sentences, objective signs of increased efforts of breathing)
    - <u>Timing</u> - in relation to exertion, position, infection, or environmental stimuli:
      - **Position:**
        - Orthopnoea - difficulty in breathing while supine often a common indicator of 1) CHF, 2) mechanical impairment of diaphragm a/w obesity
        - Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea - cardinal of CHF (although mimicked by noctural asthma)
        - Platypnea - dyspnoea in upright position w/ relief in supine position raises suspicion of left atrial myxoma or hepatopulmonary syndrome
      - **Infection** - consider infective causes if +ve for fever, cough or other URTI Sx
      - **Environmental stimuli** - probe common exposure for:
        - Asthma - animal dander, house dust mites, stuffed toys etc.
        - COPD - smoking
        - ILD - occupational exposure to organic, and inorganic dusts, asbestos etc.
  - **Associated Sx:**
  - **PMH** - probe risk factors for CAD or ILD
  - **SHx** - probe risk factors for CAD, or ILD
- **P/E** - begins during the interview through inspection of the patient:
  - **Primary assessment** - airway, breathing, circulation, disability:
    - <u>Airway</u>:
      - Ability to speak in sentences
      - Tripod position (sitting with hands braced on knees) suggestive of increased airway resistance
    - <u>Breathing</u> (details below) - formal assessment of respiratory rate and rhythm, signs of respiratory distress, and presence of pulsus paradoxus
    - <u>Circulation</u> - BP/P, capillary refill to define circulatory state
    - <u>Disability</u> - confusion suggestive of hypoxic brain injury, or hypercapnia
  - **Assessment of respiration:**
    - **Inability to speak in full sentence before gasping** - suggestive of underlying capacity results in:
      - 1\. Increased stimulation of receptors
      - 2\. Impairment of ventilation w/ reduced vital capacity
    - **Objective signs of respiratory distress:**
      - <u>Respiratory rate</u> - tachypnoea as an objective marked of increased effort of breathing
      - <u>Recruitment of accessory muscles</u> (supraclavicular muscles, intercostal muscles) - suggestive of increased work of breathing (e.g. stiffness of lung-chest wall system or increased airway resistance)
    - **Pulsus paradoxus** (\> 10 mmHg SBP drop on inspiration) - suggestive of 1) <u>pericardial disease</u>, 2) severe exacerbation of asthma or COPD resulting in <u>hyperinflation</u>
  - **General examination:**
    - <u>Pallor</u> - suggestive of anaemia
    - <u>Cyanosis</u> - central cyanosis suggestive of hypoxaemia
    - <u>Clubbing</u> - points to varying cardiovascular or respiratory pathologies
    - <u>Stigmata of chronic liver disease</u> - suggestive of cirrhosis
  - **Respiratory examination:**
    - <u>Inspection and palpation</u> - unilateral vs bilateral disease
    - <u>Percussion</u> - dull vs hyper-resonant
    - <u>Auscultation</u> - focal signs such as wheeze, coarse crackles, fine crackles
  - **Cardiovascular examination:**
    - <u>Signs of elevated right heart pressure</u> - elevated JVP, oedema, loud P2
    - <u>Signs of LV dysfunction</u> - gallop rhythm, displaced apex beat (+/- altered character)
    - <u>Signs of valvular heart disease</u> - e.g. murmurs
  - **Abdominal examination:**
    - <u>Paradoxical abdominal movement</u> - inward movement on inspiration suggestive of **diaphragmatic weakness**, while rounding of abdomen on expiration suggestive of **pulmonary oedema**
- **Ix:**
  - **CXR:**
    - <u>Cardiac assessment</u> - cardiomegaly, evidence of pulmonary oedema
    - <u>Respiratory assessment</u> - assessment of lung fields:
      - Hyperinflation
      - Pneumonia
      - ILD
      - Pleural effusions
  - **Cardiac assessment** - 12-lead ECG, echocardiogram, hsTnT, NT-pro-BNP
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Supplementary O2</u> - only administered if the resting O2 saturation is \<= 89% (N.B. correction of hypoxaemia should not completely abolish dyspnoea if the primary pathology persists)
    - <u>Dx and Tx of underlying cause</u> - esp. in setting of acute dyspnoea

## Pulmonary oedema

- **Definition** - fluid accumulation in the interstitium of the lungs, which is often dependent on the balance of hydrostatic and oncotpic forces within pulmonary capillaries and in the surrounding tissue
- **Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema:**
  - <u>Etiology</u> - all cardiac abnormalities resulting in rise in LVEDP, LA pressure, and pulmonary venous pressure (i.e. CHF)
  - <u>Pathophysiology of cardiogenic pulmonary oedema</u> - increased VQ mismatch:
    - Increased hydrostatic pressure - resulting in increased fluid movement from capillary into interstitium (or even alveolar oedema in severe cases), resulting in decreased compliance
    - Transudative pleural effusion - additional comprimise to respiratory function (+/- focal atelectasis) contributing to dyspnoea
  - <u>Mx</u> - generally correctable by supplemental O2
- **Non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema:**
  - <u>Etiology</u> - direct lung injury vs indirect lung injury vs others: 
  ![](img/non_cardiogenic_pulmonary_oedema.png)
  - <u>Pathophysiology of non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema</u> - intrapulmonary shunting and decreased compliance:
    - Damaged pulmonary capillary lining - resulting in constant leackage of oncotic molecules (e.g. albumin) into tissue, resulting in fluid entry into lungs
    - Dysfunction of surfactants - predisposes to atelactesis in low-lung volumes
  - <u>Mx</u> - generally non-responsive to high-flow supplementary O2 due to intrapulmonary shunting
